<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haliacmon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aliakmonas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Haliacmon, modern Aliakmonas, is the longest river flowing entirely within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, about 300 kilometres long. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindus">Pindus</a> mountains near the Albanian border and crosses western and central Macedonia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermaic-gulf">Thermaic Gulf</a> west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>. Its lower plain, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a> and Pella, was the heartland of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haliacmon.jpg" alt="Haliacmon" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aliakmon_locator_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
